 "claims": [
  {
   "id": "W_hook",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "Leandro Andrade was sentenced under California's three strikes law to two consecutive terms of 25 years to life (50 years to life) for two petty thefts of videotapes; one of the tapes in the first theft was 'Snow White'. Narration: 'He got 50 years to life for Snow White.' (hot-take framing; line 2 immediately adds the other eight tapes).",
   "sources": [
    "justia",
    "lii",
    "loc",
    "csm",
    "bas",
    "ljw"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "W_nine",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "Nine videotapes in all: five at a Kmart in Ontario, California (4 November 1995) and four at a Kmart in Montclair, California (18 November 1995). Narration: 'Plus eight other videotapes.'",
   "sources": [
    "justia",
    "lii",
    "loc",
    "csm",
    "wiki"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "W_waist",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "At the Montclair Kmart he placed four videotapes in the rear waistband of his pants (opinion); both thefts were at Kmart stores in San Bernardino County. Narration: 'He hid them in his waistband at two Kmarts.'",
   "sources": [
    "justia",
    "lii",
    "loc",
    "csm",
    "bas"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "W_stopped",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "Store security personnel stopped him as he was leaving each store. Shown: the guard steps in at the exit; label 'STOPPED AT THE DOOR · BOTH TIMES'.",
   "sources": [
    "justia",
    "lii",
    "loc",
    "bas"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "W_stat",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "STATISTIC (counter): the tapes were worth $84.70 and $68.84, $153.54 in all (reported as about $150 / $153). Narration: 'Total value? About 150 dollars.' Counter: $153.",
   "sources": [
    "justia",
    "lii",
    "loc",
    "npr",
    "csm",
    "ljw"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "W_priors",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "His prior record included three counts of first-degree residential burglary (1983), which counted as strikes. Narration: 'But he had prior burglary convictions.'",
   "sources": [
    "justia",
    "lii",
    "loc",
    "wiki",
    "seattle"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "W_law",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "California's three strikes law: a third felony after two prior serious or violent felony convictions brings 25 years to life; petty theft with a prior can be charged as a felony. Narration: 'So under three strikes, ...' (s05 line 1).",
   "sources": [
    "justia",
    "lii",
    "loc",
    "csm",
    "npr"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "W_each",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "Each of his two petty-theft-with-a-prior convictions triggered a separate application of the three strikes law. Narration: '... each theft counted as strike three.'",
   "sources": [
    "justia",
    "lii",
    "loc",
    "npr",
    "ljw"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "W_sentence",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "The judge imposed two consecutive terms of 25 years to life. Narration: 'Twenty-five to life, times two.' On screen: 25 + 25 = 50 YEARS TO LIFE.",
   "sources": [
    "justia",
    "lii",
    "loc",
    "npr",
    "ljw",
    "mg"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "W_lawside",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "The State of California defended the sentence under the three strikes law and the Supreme Court majority (O'Connor) upheld it. Narration: 'The law was clear: three strikes.'",
   "sources": [
    "justia",
    "lii",
    "loc",
    "ljw",
    "police1"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "W_critics",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "The Ninth Circuit had held the sentence to be cruel and unusual punishment; Justice Souter's dissent (with Stevens, Ginsburg and Breyer) argued the consecutive sentences were grossly disproportionate to two minor thefts. Narration: 'The critics said: the punishment did not fit the crime.' (paraphrase; no direct quote used).",
   "sources": [
    "lii_d",
    "justia",
    "ljw",
    "csm",
    "wiki"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "W_scotus",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "The U.S. Supreme Court reversed the Ninth Circuit and upheld the sentence 5-4 in Lockyer v. Andrade, decided 5 March 2003. Narration: 'Then the Supreme Court upheld it, five to four.'",
   "sources": [
    "justia",
    "lii",
    "loc",
    "ljw",
    "police1",
    "semo",
    "mg"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "W_golf",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "FUN FACT CARD: the same day (5 March 2003) the Court upheld, in Ewing v. California, a sentence of 25 years to life for stealing three golf clubs.",
   "sources": [
    "ljw",
    "police1",
    "semo"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "W_lesson",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "LESSON CARD (practical, for the viewer): if arrested or questioned: you may remain silent (say clearly that you are using that right); ask for a lawyer (a public defender if you cannot afford one) and do not talk without one; say clearly 'I do not consent to a search' and do not physically resist. Sources: county public defender offices (Santa Clara County CA, Solano County CA, Dutchess County NY, Lancaster County NE). Rows: 'SAY YOU WILL STAY SILENT', 'ASK FOR A LAWYER', 'DO NOT CONSENT TO SEARCHES'.",
   "sources": [
    "pd_sc",
    "pd_sol",
    "pd_dut",
    "pd_lan"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "W_parole",
   "type": "established",
   "nature": "fact",
   "text": "As sentenced, he would not be eligible for parole until 2046, when he would be 87. Narration: 'His first shot at parole? At 87 years old.' Card: AS SENTENCED. (A later release after Proposition 36 of 2012 is reported by one source only and contradicted by an older one, so it is NOT claimed.)",
   "sources": [
    "wiki",
    "bas",
    "csm"
   ]
  }
 ],
